<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap-text wrap-text--intro center">
    <p class="eyebrow">Georgia Institute of Technology</p>
    <h1>Welcome to the Stroud Lab</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      We watch evolution and ecology happening in the wild, and connect what an
      individual lizard is doing to why whole communities are assembled the way
      they are.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule-wrap"><hr></div>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="photo-hover" tabindex="0">
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/sagrei-dewlap-rock.jpg" alt="A brown anole extending its orange dewlap on a rock">
        <span class="photo-hover__caption"><span>
          A brown anole, <em>Anolis sagrei</em>, extends its dewlap on Lizard Island.
        </span></span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme I</p>
        <h2>Measuring natural selection in the wild</h2>
        <p>
          Our long-term study on Lizard Island asks how natural selection actually
          operates across individuals, species, space, and time — not in theory but
          as a measurable process in a real community. The community has five
          species: two semi-terrestrial specialists, two arboreal canopy specialists,
          and one large trunk specialist. We sample all of them, between 800 and
          1,000 lizards at any given time, every spring and every fall.
        </p>
        <p>
          That schedule is the point. Sampling twice a year lets us measure selection
          through the summer reproductive season and separately through the winter
          non-reproductive season, resolving variation that an annual census would
          average away. The study began in Fall 2015 and has run since, with a forced
          hiatus during COVID.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ambition for the project is explicit: we are modelling it on the Grants'
          long-term study of Darwin's finches in the Galápagos, and we intend it to
          become one of the best known evolutionary field studies in the world.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/research/">More on natural selection in the wild</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule-wrap"><hr></div>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split split--reverse">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme II</p>
        <h2>The evolution of thermal ecophysiology</h2>
        <p>
          How do ecology and behavior shape the evolution of thermal physiology?
          We approach this from two directions.
        </p>
        <p>
          The first is a gradient. Green anoles reached south Florida from tropical
          Cuba about 5 to 7 million years ago and now live from the subtropics to
          temperate Tennessee and North Carolina. Our initial work suggests the
          physiological ceilings are not what changed: lower thermal limits differ
          surprisingly little across the range. Behavior is what differs.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second is speed. How fast can any of this happen? The tropical lizards
          that have arrived in Florida over the past century give us contemporary
          introductions with known start dates, which makes it possible to explore
          acclimatization from adaptation. We combine ecological, physiological,
          genomic, and transcriptomic approaches.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/research/">More on thermal ecophysiology</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo-hover" tabindex="0">
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/carolinensis-grass.jpg" alt="A green anole perched on a blade of grass">
        <span class="photo-hover__caption"><span>
          A green anole, <em>Anolis carolinensis</em>, in the field.
        </span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule-wrap"><hr></div>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split">
      <div class="photo-hover" tabindex="0">
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/field-canopy.jpg" alt="Researcher working in a tropical forest canopy">
        <span class="photo-hover__caption"><span>
          Fieldwork in the canopy at Lizard Island, Miami.
        </span></span>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme III</p>
        <h2>New technology for eco-evolutionary field studies</h2>
        <p>
          The limiting factor in evolutionary ecology is not ideas but data. We can
          only measure what we can observe, and the biology of wild animals mostly
          happens faster, smaller, or further away than we can follow by hand.
        </p>
        <p>
          We develop new tools to expand what is observable: automated
          mark-recapture systems, thermal imaging from drones, computer-vision
          pipelines for morphology at scale. The ambition is not technology for its
          own sake but to collect the kind of longitudinal, individual-level,
          multi-species data that the biggest questions in evolutionary ecology
          actually require.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/software/">Software & tools from the lab</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="rule-wrap"><hr></div>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="split split--reverse">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Theme IV</p>
        <h2>Introduced species as natural experiments</h2>
        <p>
          Introduced species are often framed as problems to be solved. We also
          treat them as opportunities to be understood. A species that has colonized
          a new continent in the past century is a replicated, natural experiment
          in evolution and community assembly, running in real time.
        </p>
        <p>
          The introduced anoles of Florida — up to eight species in a single park —
          let us ask how communities are structured, how species partition resources
          when they have not co-evolved, and how fast morphology and behavior can
          respond to new ecological pressures. This work also underpins our
          long-running Miami Lizards citizen science project.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/miami-lizards/">Miami Lizards project</a></p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo-hover" tabindex="0">
        <img src="/assets/img/photos/sagrei-trunk.jpg" alt="A brown anole on a tree trunk">
        <span class="photo-hover__caption"><span>
          Brown anoles, <em>Anolis sagrei</em>, are one of Florida's most successful introductions.
        </span></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

